! conclusion: Co-Design Is Not Consultation

  + context: You are involving the people affected by your research, and want the involvement to be real.

  + IF:
    Your project brings in patients, service users, or community members, and you have a choice about what their involvement means.

  + HOWEVER:
    "Involvement" collapses several distinct things into one word. Directing the research (deciding what is worth studying, how it is run, what counts as a good outcome) is not the same activity as making something together, and treating them as interchangeable produces the tick-box version of both — a panel consulted late, an idea rubber-stamped, a partner thanked in the acknowledgements.

  + THEN:
    Name which one you are doing, in writing, at the design stage. Keep involvement-in-direction and co-creation as separate strands with separate participants, methods, and outputs where you can. Budget the relationship-building time explicitly — it is the method, not the overhead — and expect partners to correct you ("I see what you're trying to say, but it's not like that") rather than agreeing.

  + BECAUSE:
    The two activities answer different questions and fail in different ways. Kept distinct, each can be done properly and evidenced; conflated, the more demanding one silently degrades into the cheaper one, and the resulting programme is shaped by the research team after all.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Write one sentence into the project plan naming which strand each partner group belongs to.]
    next[For a worked example of a peer-conceived, peer-led programme where the distinction was held deliberately, contact the First Steps team in Sport, Nutrition and Allied Health Professions.]
